summary_en.technical_approach:
The DHRCL framework includes three core designs:
1. Four-layer dense reward system: syntax validity (fraction of valid syntactic units extracted by an error-tolerant parser), execution success (binary indicator of runtime completion), unit test pass rate (dense functional signal for partial correctness), and AST structural similarity (auxiliary reference-aligned structural signal, not used as a measure of semantic equivalence or overall code quality);
2. Three-stage curriculum learning: optimization focus shifts in the order Syntax → Execution → Pass & Structural, with stage duration automatically determined from held-out validation performance trends, no manual syntax or execution capability thresholds required;
3. Stage-aware token-level credit redistribution: confidence-weighted updates in the syntax stage, uniform weighting for execution feedback, and uncertainty-weighted updates in the final stage, to allocate trajectory-level rewards appropriately across generated tokens.
